// Shell blocks: effects through the host seam.
//
// `#!{ ... }` embeds flat commands; each lowers to a `perform` of the host
// `Exec` effect, and the shell driver's handlers (Exec/Fs/Proc/Env) answer
// them. The reply is ordinary typed data -- a record with `stdout`,
// `stderr`, and `exit_code` fields -- not magic captured text: "tools are an
// effect signature" is the design stance, and this is its smallest witness.
//
// (Pipelines inside `#!{ }` are deliberately still rejected -- the pipeline
// surface belongs to a later semantic stage; see the pathological tree.)
#!{ printf 'hello from the host\n'; }

// The harness checks (shell mode runs a single top-level item):
//@ mode: shell
//@ expect: clean
//@ expect-stdout-contains: hello from the host
